# char_test manifest — the policy that lets a client speak to the serial
# port at all (#497).
#
# 🔑 A CLIENT OF char_server NEEDED ONE, AND NOTHING IN THIS TREE HAD ONE.
# ush, the only other tty client, carries an empty manifest and reaches its
# terminal through the unbound-ctty exception -- which asks proc_server who
# the session leader is.  proc_server has not crossed to x86-64, so that
# exception is closed on this target and the cap-checked road is the only
# one: char_server verifies the token on every read and every write.
#
# ⚠️ Without this file the test does not fail quietly, it fails LOUDLY, and
# that is on purpose.  cap_request() comes back refused and the test says so
# and stops -- rather than carrying on with an empty token, which would
# exercise char_server's refusal path and report it as the serial port.

name char_test

# RESOURCE_SERIAL (7), any tty, CHAR_CAP_TTY_RW (1<<1 = 0x2).
#
# ⚠️ `any' and not the device id, for the same reason cap_test says `any'
# about block devices: the id is assigned in the order modules attach, so a
# manifest that named 1 would be a policy about whichever device happened to
# be first.  The test asks char_server which device is the tty and requests a
# capability for that one; this line says it may hold such a capability at
# all.
required 7 any 0x2

# No `delegatable' lines: this task hands nothing on.
